Let's also define dying as actually showing signs of deterioration displaying artefacts incorrect geometry culling or facing or improper stretching of shapes, making too much noise, getting hotter than normal under very light loads and stuff like that instead of something like: "Oh boy! My GPU is old... Must be dying!!!"

 @rdmz135  
How do you get faster performance without upping energy requirements?
You literally can't! There are changes to how much electricity is used in all overclocking scenarios. This is just how transistors works on a fundamental level. All overclocking affects power requirements and may lead to eloctromigration which shortens the useful lifespan of semiconductors. This is really bad because all a CPU, GPU and RAM is are massive groups of semiconductors sandwiched between silicon!

 @FlawlessWorldAces  Did you see the video that Steve at Gamers Nexus did on Vega 56? 

Not saying that 2070 isn't better than Vega, but you can buy 2 Vega cards for less than a 2080 TI and get better performance.

This launch was a bad launch. You cant even take advantage of RTX yet.

 @seaofrage  You're right that I won't get much of a boost out of an 1060, but right now I'm just looking for a cheap replacement that isn't worse than the failing 970, and lets me game on 1080p with 60 fps (my current monitor) after turning some unnecessary and/or processing-intensive settings down a notch (shadows) or off (DoF, motion blur). It's only supposed to last for a year, if even that. The plan is to then do a big upgrade (4k monitor, highest-end consumer CPU and graphics card), when the next-gen hardware is out. That should then last for 6 years with perhaps a GPU switch after 3.

I actually planned that big upgrade for this year, but hardware development on all the key parts of my plan has been disappointing. The 2080 Ti and Intel's 9900k are both held back by the inability to produce in 10nm, the result is hardware that is stretched to the limit to still eke a halfway decent performance gain out of it. The thermals and power draw don't look like something that would hold 5 years under my heavy usage scenarios. And even the new high-end monitors still have teething problems (like the requirement of noisy fans that defeat the purpose of my low-noise PC build). So it seems really best to just get by cheaply for the next year and then strike.

Thanks for your feedback though, always appreciated!

